.he "lex (1)                      8/18/26                      lex (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
lex
- generate programs for simple lexical tasks
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
lex [ -rctvfn ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Lex generates programs to be used in simple lexical analysis of text.
The input files hold strings and expressions to be searched for, and C
text to be executed when one is found.  A file lex.yy.c is generated
which, loaded with the library, copies input to output except when a
string in the file is found; then the corresponding program text is
executed.  The actual string matched is left in yytext, an external
character array.  Matching is done in the order of the strings in the
file.
.sp
Strings may contain square brackets for character classes, as in
.nf
	[abx-z]
.fi
which means a, b, x, y and z; the operators *, + and ? mean any
non-negative number of, any positive number of, and zero or one
occurrences of the previous character or class.  A dot is the class of
all ASCII characters except newline.  Parentheses group and a vertical
bar is alternation.  A leading ^ permits a match only immediately after
a newline, and a trailing $ requires one.  A / in an expression marks
trailing context: only the part before the slash is returned in yytext,
but the remainder must follow in the input.  An operator character may
be used as an ordinary symbol inside "" or preceded by \.
.sp
Three macros are expected: input() to read a character, unput(c) to
replace one, and output(c) to write one.  They are defined in terms of
the standard streams, but may be overridden.  The program generated is
yylex(), and the library holds a main() which calls it.  REJECT on the
right of a rule rejects the match and tries the next suitable one;
yymore() accumulates more characters into yytext; yyless(p) pushes back
the part of the match from p on.  input and output use the files yyin
and yyout, which default to stdin and stdout.
.sp
Any line beginning with a blank is taken as C text and copied; before
the first %% it goes into the external definition area of lex.yy.c.
All rules follow a %%, as in yacc.  A line before the %% beginning with
a non-blank character defines the string on its left to be the rest of
the line; it is called out later by surrounding it with {}.  Curly
brackets do not imply parentheses; only string substitution is done.
Example:
.nf
	D	[0-9]
	%%
	if	printf("IF statement\n");
	[a-z]+	printf("tag, value %s\n", yytext);
	0{D}+	printf("octal number %s\n", yytext);
	{D}+	printf("decimal number %s\n", yytext);
	"++"	printf("unary op\n");
	"+"	printf("binary op\n");
.fi
.sp
The external names lex generates all begin with yy or YY.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-r	Ratfor actions.

.ti -10
-c	C actions; the default.

.ti -10
-t	Write lex.yy.c to the standard output rather than to a file.

.ti -10
-v	Print a one-line summary of the machine generated.

.ti -10
-f	Faster compilation: no packing, for much smaller machines only.

.ti -10
-n	Suppress the summary.

.in -10
.sp
The flags must come before any files.  Multiple files are treated as a
single file; with no file the standard input is used.
.sp
In the definitions section %p num, %n num, %t num and %a num set the
maximum number of positions, states, parse tree nodes and transitions,
defaults 2000, 500, 1000 and 3000.  Using any of them implies -v unless
-n is given.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
